Task 1: Install and Configure Red Hat Satellite 6
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the System Environment
First, we need to ensure our system meets the requirements and is properly configured.

Connect to your lab machine and verify system requirements:
# Check system resources
free -h
df -h
cat /etc/redhat-release
Set the hostname for your Satellite server:
# Set a fully qualified domain name (FQDN)
hostnamectl set-hostname satellite.example.com

# Verify the hostname
hostnamectl status
Configure the hosts file:
# Edit the hosts file
vi /etc/hosts

# Add the following line (replace with your actual IP)
192.168.1.100 satellite.example.com satellite
Disable and stop firewalld (for lab purposes):
systemctl stop firewalld
systemctl disable firewalld
Disable SELinux temporarily for installation:
setenforce 0
sed -i 's/SELINUX=enforcing/SELINUX=permissive/' /etc/selinux/config
Subtask 1.2: Register System and Enable Repositories
Register your system with Red Hat Subscription Management:
# Register with your Red Hat credentials
subscription-manager register --username your_username --password your_password

# List available subscriptions
subscription-manager list --available

# Attach Red Hat Satellite subscription (use the pool ID from the list)
subscription-manager attach --pool=POOL_ID_HERE
Enable required repositories:
# Enable Satellite repositories
subscription-manager repos --enable=rhel-8-for-x86_64-baseos-rpms \
--enable=rhel-8-for-x86_64-appstream-rpms \
--enable=satellite-6.14-for-rhel-8-x86_64-rpms \
--enable=satellite-maintenance-6.14-for-rhel-8-x86_64-rpms

# Update the system
dnf update -y
Subtask 1.3: Install Red Hat Satellite
Install the Satellite installer package:
# Install satellite package
dnf install satellite -y
Run the Satellite installer:
# Execute the installer with basic configuration
satellite-installer --scenario satellite \
--foreman-initial-admin-username admin \
--foreman-initial-admin-password redhat123 \
--foreman-initial-organization "Default Organization" \
--foreman-initial-location "Default Location"
Note: The installation process may take 30-60 minutes depending on your system performance.

Verify the installation:
# Check Satellite services status
satellite-maintain service status

# Verify web interface accessibility
curl -k https://satellite.example.com
Subtask 1.4: Initial Satellite Configuration
Access the Satellite web interface:

Open a web browser and navigate to: https://satellite.example.com
Login with username: admin and password: redhat123
Complete the initial setup wizard:

Navigate through the welcome screens
Configure your organization settings
Set up initial location parameters
Task 2: Understand Satellite Architecture Components
Subtask 2.1: Explore Foreman Component
Foreman is the core provisioning and configuration management component of Satellite.

Access Foreman features through the web interface:

Navigate to Hosts → All Hosts
Explore Configure → Host Groups
Review Infrastructure → Compute Resources
Examine Foreman configuration files:

# View main Foreman configuration
cat /etc/foreman/settings.yaml

# Check Foreman database configuration
cat /etc/foreman/database.yml
Verify Foreman services:
# Check Foreman-related services
systemctl status foreman
systemctl status httpd
Subtask 2.2: Understand Katello Component
Katello provides content management capabilities including repository synchronization and content views.

Explore Katello features in the web interface:

Navigate to Content → Products
Check Content → Content Views
Review Content → Lifecycle Environments
Examine Katello configuration:

# View Katello configuration
ls -la /etc/katello/

# Check Katello database settings
cat /etc/katello/katello.conf
Subtask 2.3: Investigate Pulp Component
Pulp handles repository management and content storage for Satellite.

Check Pulp services and configuration:
# Verify Pulp services
systemctl status pulpcore-api
systemctl status pulpcore-content
systemctl status pulpcore-worker@*

# View Pulp configuration
cat /etc/pulp/settings.py
Explore Pulp storage:
# Check Pulp content storage
ls -la /var/lib/pulp/
du -sh /var/lib/pulp/media/
Subtask 2.4: Examine Smart Proxy Functionality
Smart Proxy provides various infrastructure services like DNS, DHCP, and TFTP.

Check Smart Proxy services:
# Verify Smart Proxy status
systemctl status foreman-proxy

# View Smart Proxy configuration
cat /etc/foreman-proxy/settings.yml
Explore Smart Proxy features in the web interface:
Navigate to Infrastructure → Smart Proxies
Click on your Satellite server to view enabled features
Review available proxy features and their status
Subtask 2.5: Understanding Capsule Servers
Capsule Servers extend Satellite functionality to remote locations.

View Capsule information:

In the web interface, go to Infrastructure → Capsules
Note that your main Satellite server also functions as a Capsule
Check Capsule-related services:

# Services that would run on Capsule servers
systemctl status squid
systemctl status tftp
Task 3: Register Satellite to Red Hat Subscription Management
Subtask 3.1: Verify Current Registration Status
Check current subscription status:
# View current registration
subscription-manager status

# List consumed subscriptions
subscription-manager list --consumed

# Check available repositories
subscription-manager repos --list-enabled
Subtask 3.2: Configure Satellite for Content Synchronization
Create a manifest for your Satellite:

Log into Red Hat Customer Portal (access.redhat.com)
Navigate to Subscriptions → Subscription Allocations
Create a new allocation for your Satellite
Download the manifest file
Upload the manifest to Satellite:

In Satellite web interface, go to Content → Subscriptions
Click Manage Manifest
Upload your downloaded manifest file
Subtask 3.3: Enable and Synchronize Content
Enable Red Hat repositories:

Navigate to Content → Red Hat Repositories
Enable desired repositories (e.g., RHEL 8 BaseOS, AppStream)
Click Enable Selected Repositories
Create a sync plan:

# Create a sync plan via CLI
hammer sync-plan create \
--name "Daily Sync" \
--description "Daily repository synchronization" \
--interval daily \
--sync-date "2024-01-01 02:00:00" \
--enabled true \
--organization "Default Organization"
Start initial synchronization:
Go to Content → Products
Select Red Hat Enterprise Linux for x86_64
Click Sync Now for immediate synchronization
Subtask 3.4: Verify Synchronization Status
Monitor sync progress:
# Check sync status via CLI
hammer repository list --organization "Default Organization"

# View sync status
hammer sync-plan info --name "Daily Sync" --organization "Default Organization"
Verify content availability:
In web interface, navigate to Content → Products
Check that repositories show package counts
Verify last sync timestamps
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Installation Fails Due to Insufficient Resources
Solution:

# Check available memory and disk space
free -h
df -h

# If insufficient, increase VM resources or clean up space
dnf clean all
Issue 2: Web Interface Not Accessible
Solution:

# Check httpd service
systemctl status httpd
systemctl restart httpd

# Verify firewall settings
firewall-cmd --list-all
Issue 3: Subscription Registration Problems
Solution:

# Clean subscription data and re-register
subscription-manager clean
subscription-manager register --username your_username --password your_password
